{
  "$schema": "../../catalog/skill.spec.schema.json",
  "id": "rag-local-with-container",
  "domain": "ai-vector-and-rag",
  "value": ["not-in-training-data", "corrects-a-wrong-assumption", "multi-step-with-silent-failures"],
  "correction": "Agents hold one of two wrong beliefs about prototyping retrieval augmented generation on the local Azure SQL Database container: either that it is a toy and the work has to start against a cloud endpoint with a key, or that because it is the same engine anything proved locally holds in Azure SQL Database. Measured on 2026-08-28 by running one script against both, with the same corpus and the same locally produced vectors, the storage and retrieval half is identical: the same cosine distances to six decimal places, the same top three in the same order, a DiskANN vector index that built on both, approximate search returning the same rows, and the same error text throughout. So the offline prototype is real. But three things break, and every one of them breaks in the direction that flatters the laptop. The container enforces no outbound domain allowlist and returned HTTP 200 from a public non-Azure host while the cloud refused the identical call with Msg 31612 before DNS, so an endpoint proved locally can be refused by policy on its first cloud run. Row level security and a vector index cannot coexist on the container in either order, Msg 37579 creating the policy after the index and Msg 42244 creating the index after the policy, while both succeed in the cloud, so the tenant predicate on the chunk table that is the whole defence against a retrieval leaking text the asking user may not read cannot be rehearsed locally on an indexed table. Managed identity is refused on the container with Msg 31644 naming an sp_configure remedy that the container itself refuses with Msg 40510, so the production credential has no local rehearsal. A fourth difference is not the engine at all and costs the most: the code moves unchanged but the corpus does not, because a local embedding model and a hosted one are different geometries and usually different dimensions, and a vector column's dimension cannot be changed by ALTER COLUMN even on an empty table. Being wrong costs a retrieval query that passes every local test and is refused in the cloud, an isolation control that ships having never been exercised, and a full re-embed plus a new column discovered after the schema is deployed.",
  "value_probe": {
    "gate": "The answer splits the plan into the parts the local container proves faithfully and the parts where it flatters the laptop, and names which pieces have to be proved again against the service, instead of declaring the two equivalent or declaring the container unusable.",
    "task": "I am starting a document question and answer feature and my Azure subscription request is stuck in procurement for a few weeks. I have the Azure SQL Database container running on my laptop and a small embedding model I can run locally, and I want to build as much of this as possible offline so that when the subscription lands I am mostly deploying rather than starting.\n\nThe plan is to load about 2000 chunks, store the vectors, build the retrieval query with the index in place, add per customer isolation on the chunk table so one customer can never retrieve another's text, and wire it so that in production the database itself calls the hosted embedding endpoint using the application's managed identity. I would test all of that on the laptop first.\n\nBefore I spend two weeks on this, tell me which parts of that plan I can genuinely prove locally and which parts will behave differently once it moves to the service.",
    "states": [
      "Vector storage, distance results, index build and ranked retrieval behave the same on the container and on the service, so that half of the prototype is real.",
      "The per customer predicate cannot be put on an indexed chunk table on the container, so that isolation control cannot be rehearsed locally and must be proved against the service.",
      "The container places no restriction on which outbound hosts it will call, while the service calls only a fixed set, so an endpoint proved locally can still be refused on its first cloud run.",
      "Managed identity authentication is not available on the container, so the production credential has no local rehearsal.",
      "The code moves but the corpus does not: vectors from a local model have to be regenerated against the hosted model, and the column's declared dimension cannot be changed afterwards."
    ],
    "refutes": [
      "The local Azure SQL Database container and the service behave identically, so anything that passes locally needs no re-testing after deployment.",
      "The local container is only good for syntax checking and cannot store vectors, build the retrieval index or reproduce the service's ranking.",
      "A per tenant predicate can be placed on an indexed chunk table on the container, so the isolation control can be exercised offline.",
      "Managed identity authentication to an external endpoint can be exercised against the local container.",
      "Any embedding endpoint that answers successfully from the container will also be reachable from Azure SQL Database.",
      "Embeddings produced by the local model can be kept as they are once the corpus moves to a hosted embedding model, because vectors are just numbers."
    ]
  },
  "posture": ["read", "write", "execute"],
  "maturity": "draft",
  "applies_to": ["azure-sql-db-container", "azure-sql-db"],
  "related": [
    "rag-on-azure-sql",
    "vector-search-azure-sql",
    "embeddings-and-external-models",
    "langchain-and-llamaindex-on-azure-sql",
    "dev-container-templates",
    "connect-to-azure-sql",
    "deploy-app-to-azure",
    "azuresql-db-rag",
    "azuresql-db-container",
    "azuresql-db-local-to-cloud"
  ],
  "validation": {
    "target": "both",
    "setup": [
      "the local Azure SQL Database container with a provisioned user database",
      "a local embedding model reachable from application code, and a local generation model for the grounding step",
      "an Azure SQL Database to move to, for the checks that cannot run locally"
    ],
    "assert": [
      "one dimension number appears in the schema, in the local embedding call and in the cloud model's requested dimensions, and a test fails when they disagree",
      "the embedding call is a single function with one endpoint in it",
      "the retrieval query text is identical in both environments, filter included, and was executed against both",
      "nothing in the local path requires an outbound call, and the same results are reached with the machine offline",
      "no key, endpoint host name or account name is committed, and the cloud identity is a managed identity",
      "every endpoint the design depends on was checked against the allowlist from a cloud database rather than from the container",
      "the tenant or permission predicate was exercised on an unindexed local table or in the cloud, and a test fails when the policy is absent",
      "the corpus was fully re-embedded after the model changed, and grouping by the model column returns exactly one row",
      "every sample corpus is fabricated and contains no customer text or real identifier"
    ]
  },
  "triggering": {
    "explicit": [
      "Use the rag-local-with-container skill",
